<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Julius Nyerere</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1922">1922</a>–<a href="/years/1999">1999</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Julius Nyerere was a Tanzanian teacher and politician who led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanganyika-african-national-union">Tanganyika African National Union</a> to independence in 1961. He became Prime Minister and then President of Tanganyika, and in 1964 united it with Zanzibar to form Tanzania, which he led until 1985. He promoted African socialism through ujamaa villages, built national unity around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a> and supported liberation movements in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. In 1979 his army overthrew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idi-amin">Idi Amin</a> in Uganda.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/julius-nyerere.jpg" alt="Julius Nyerere" width="320" height="470" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rob Mieremet / Anefo · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:President_Nyerere_van_Tanzania,_koppen,_Bestanddeelnr_928-2879_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
